   "source": [
    "---\n",
    "title: \"MaxDiff Analysis of MSBA Class Preferences\"\n",
    "subtitle: \"Counts, MLE, and Bayesian estimation of the MNL model\"\n",
    "author: \"Yixin Cao\"\n",
    "date: May 28, 2026\n",
    "---\n",
    "\n",
    "\n",
    "## Introduction\n",
    "\n",
    "MaxDiff, also called Best-Worst Scaling, is a survey method used to measure relative preference. In each task, a respondent sees a small subset of items, in this case four MSBA classes, and chooses the item they prefer most and the item they prefer least. Because respondents repeatedly make best-worst choices, MaxDiff creates direct trade-offs between the items.\n",
    "\n",
    "MaxDiff is useful because people are usually better at making comparisons than assigning numeric ratings. A 1–10 rating scale can be hard to interpret because different people use rating scales differently. MaxDiff forces respondents to choose winners and losers within each set, which reduces scale-use bias and creates clearer preference differences.\n",
    "\n",
    "In this assignment, the ten items are the ten core MSBA classes at UCSD Rady. I estimate class preferences in three ways. First, I use a simple counts analysis based on the percentage of times each class was picked best minus the percentage of times it was picked worst. Second, I estimate a multinomial logit model using maximum likelihood. Third, I estimate the same MNL model using Bayesian methods with a Metropolis-Hastings sampler.\n",
    "\n",
    "I expect the three methods to agree on the general ranking of classes, especially for the most-preferred and least-preferred items. They may differ more in the middle of the ranking, where preferences are closer and small differences can change the order.\n",
    "\n",
    "\n",
    "\n",
    "\n",
    "## The Data"
   ]
