    "The original survey contained 1,275 task observations. After applying the MaxDiff validation rules, 680 tasks remained for analysis. Each retained task contained exactly four displayed classes, one best choice, and one worst choice.\n",
    "\n",
    "The resulting `task_data` dataset contains one row per valid MaxDiff task and stores:\n",
    "\n",
    "- The four classes shown in the task\n",
    "- The class selected as most preferred (best)\n",
    "- The class selected as least preferred (worst)\n",
    "\n",
    "This format is convenient for MNL estimation because each row contains all information needed to evaluate the likelihood contribution of a respondent's observed choices. The final dataset of 680 valid tasks serves as the input for both the maximum likelihood and Bayesian MNL models.\n",
    "\n",
    "\n",
    "## MNL via Maximum Likelihood\n",
    "\n",
    "The multinomial logit (MNL) model estimates a utility coefficient for each class. Classes with larger utility coefficients are more likely to be selected as the best choice. The model uses both the best and worst selections from each MaxDiff task.\n",
    "\n",
    "The log-likelihood for the MaxDiff MNL model is:\n",
    "\n",
    "$$\n",
    "\\ell(\\boldsymbol{\\beta})\n",
    "=\n",
    "\\sum_{\\text{tasks}}\n",
    "\\left[\n",
    "\\beta_{j_b^*}\n",
    "-\n",
    "\\log \\sum_{j \\in \\text{shown}} \\exp(\\beta_j)\n",
    "-\n",
    "\\beta_{j_w^*}\n",
    "-\n",
    "\\log \\sum_{j \\in \\text{shown}\\setminus\\{j_b^*\\}}\n",
    "\\exp(-\\beta_j)\n",
    "\\right]\n",
    "$$\n",
    "\n",
    "where:\n",
    "\n",
    "- $j_b^*$ is the item selected as best\n",
    "- $j_w^*$ is the item selected as worst\n",
    "\n",
    "The first term models the best choice from the full set of four classes. The second term models the worst choice after removing the selected best item.\n",
    "\n",
    "To identify the model, the utility of Class 1 is fixed at zero and the remaining nine utility coefficients are estimated."
   ]
