   "source": [
    "Across all three methods, the results are remarkably consistent. Marketing Analytics is clearly the most preferred class, ranking first in the counts analysis, MLE model, and Bayesian model. Business Analytics and Customer Analytics consistently rank second and third, while Analytics Consulting remains the least preferred class across all approaches. The agreement between the rankings suggests that students have strong and stable preferences regarding the MSBA core curriculum.\n",
    "\n",
    "The counts analysis provides a straightforward descriptive summary of preferences based on observed best and worst selections. The MNL model extends this approach by explicitly modeling the choice process and incorporating information from every item shown in each task, not only the items selected. As a result, the MNL model produces utility estimates and shares of preference that are grounded in a formal behavioral framework.\n",
    "\n",
    "The Bayesian MNL model produces estimates that are nearly identical to the MLE results, indicating that the observed data contain a strong preference signal and that the weakly informative prior has minimal influence on the final rankings. The primary advantage of the Bayesian approach is the ability to quantify uncertainty through posterior credible intervals, providing additional insight into the reliability of the estimated preferences.\n",
    "\n",
    "Overall, the consistency across the counts analysis, maximum likelihood estimation, and Bayesian estimation suggests that the preference ranking is robust and not driven by any particular estimation method. This increases confidence that the identified ranking reflects genuine student preferences rather than methodological artifacts.\n",
    "\n",
    "## Discussion\n",
    "\n",
    "This analysis shows that MSBA students have clear differences in their preferences across the ten core classes. Marketing Analytics received the strongest preference signal, while Analytics Consulting received the weakest preference signal in this survey.\n",
    "\n",
    "The three methods serve different purposes. Counts analysis is best for a quick descriptive summary. MLE MNL is better for formal model-based estimation. Bayesian MNL is useful when uncertainty is important, especially for quantities like preference shares.\n",
    "\n",
    "There are several limitations. The survey reflects the preferences of this sample of MSBA students, so the results may not generalize to all students. Preferences may also depend on prior coursework, career goals, instructors, or the quarter in which students took the survey.\n",
    "\n",
    "If I had more time, I would extend the model to estimate individual-level preferences or compare preferences across student segments. I would also test whether students with more technical backgrounds prefer different classes than students with more business-oriented backgrounds."
